Identity verification systems integrate cryptographically-signed temporal constraints by:  
(1) generating a **short-lived, single-use token** (e.g., TOTP or HMAC-based) tied to a verified recovery channel;  
(2) embedding a **timestamp and expiration window** (e.g., 5–10 minutes);  
(3) **signing with a server-side key** to ensure integrity;  
(4) requiring **user-initiated request via authenticated channel**.  

Upon submission, the system validates: **signature, time window, and one-time use** before permitting reset. This ensures only the legitimate user can act within a secure, time-bound window—preventing replay, impersonation, and abuse. Mandatory enforcement of NIST standards, real-time validation, and immediate token revocation are critical for operational security and compliance.

